        "original_id": "558b64cf-02b5-5b8c-8363-7eb754e6e545",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Places: Fulton Ferry, Brooklyn; New York City\n- Structures: Brooklyn Bridge\n- People: Joseph Stella\n- Organizations: Newark Museum\n- Topics: American art; Futurism\n\nFacts:\n- The Brooklyn Bridge in New York City is one of the oldest bridges in the United States and is a hybrid cable-stayed and suspension bridge.\n- Fulton Ferry in Brooklyn is bisected overhead by the Brooklyn Bridge.\n- Joseph Stella, born in 1877 and died in 1946, was an Italian-born American Futurist painter best known for depictions of industrial America, especially the Brooklyn Bridge.\n- The Newark Museum’s American art collection includes works by Joseph Stella along with artists such as Hiram Powers, Thomas Cole, John Singer Sargent, Albert Bierstadt, Frederick Church, Childe Hassam, Mary Cassatt, Edward Hopper, Georgia O'Keeffe, Tony Smith, and Frank Stella.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "a9ac970f-50e1-5486-a0c5-708dc361ec60",
        "text": "Page: Fulton Ferry, Brooklyn. Sentence: The district is bisected overhead by the Brooklyn Bridge.\nPage: Brooklyn Bridge. Sentence: The Brooklyn Bridge is a hybrid cable-stayed/suspension bridge in New York City and is one of the oldest bridges in the United States.\nPage: Joseph Stella. Sentence: Joseph Stella (born Giuseppe Michele Stella, June 13, 1877 – November 5, 1946) was an Italian-born American Futurist painter best known for his depictions of industrial America, especially his images of the Brooklyn Bridge.\nPage: Newark Museum. Sentence: Its extensive collections of American art include works by Hiram Powers, Thomas Cole, John Singer Sargent, Albert Bierstadt, Frederick Church, Childe Hassam, Mary Cassatt, Edward Hopper, Georgia O'Keeffe, Joseph Stella, Tony Smith and Frank Stella.",
